# Rank 1119 / CVE-2017-8827: GeniXCMS's forgot-password request handler
# accepted an unlimited number of requests with no throttle, letting an
# attacker either exhaust a target's password-reset capacity as a denial of
# service or brute-force the reset token itself across enough requests
# (CWE-287, matches CWE-307's shape exactly).
#
# forgotpassword.php's `if (isset($_POST['forgotpass']))` block validated the
# token and the CAPTCHA if enabled, then generated and emailed a reset token
# with nothing standing between an HTTP request and that generation.
#
# Pool commit f7b4a8278cdc is the real fix (a large "Version 1.1.2" release
# commit, but forgotpassword.php's own hunk is exactly this fix and nothing
# else): it adds `if (!User::lastRequestPassword()) { $alertDanger[] = 'You
# had reached request password Limit!'; }` immediately after the existing
# validation, before the CAPTCHA check, gating the rest of the block on a
# per-user request-rate check. Corpus correction, in place: the pool row's
# owner was `semplon`, an old GitHub username the advisory's own repository,
# `genixcms/genixcms`, redirects through; both names resolve to the same
# repository, so this was a rename rather than a mislink, and the row is
# normalized to the canonical `GeniXCMS` org rather than left on the
# redirect.
#
# ATTENTION, and a real binding-coverage gap rather than a missing concept.
# code.RateLimitRequiredOp (CWE_307) already exists for exactly this
# weakness, its reviewCondition is worded almost verbatim for it, "an
# authentication-sensitive endpoint accepts attempts without a throttle", and
# VYQL-AUTH-105 already consumes it against code.RateLimitCheck across
# roughly two dozen function and route-path names, forgot_password and
# password_reset among them. But every one of those bindings, and every
# RateLimitCheck control binding, is Python-only, keyed on
# `node.analysis ~= "function.context"`, a feature built around Python's
# function definitions. GeniXCMS's forgotpassword.php has no function at all;
# the file itself is the endpoint, procedural PHP with the handler logic
# inline at module scope. Extending this pair to PHP means answering two
# questions together, what a "rate-limit-requiring endpoint" looks like for a
# procedural script (a filename convention? a POST-handling `if` block at
# module scope?) and what a generic-enough PHP throttle-check control pattern
# is that would not repeat this project's own `User::lastRequestPassword()`
# name, and getting the second wrong risks reporting every correctly-throttled
# PHP endpoint that uses different naming.
#
# No definition change attempted.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-vocabulary-gap, both revisions rejected.

test "rank1119: the forgot-password handler has no rate-limit check"
  lang php
  file forgotpassword.php
  reject_review code.RateLimitRequiredOp
  code
  ```
  <?php
  if (isset($_POST['forgotpass'])) {
      $alertDanger = array();
      if (!Token::exist($_POST['token'])) {
          $alertDanger[] = TOKEN_NOT_EXIST;
      }
      if (Xaptcha::isEnable()) {
          if (!isset($_POST['g-recaptcha-response']) || $_POST['g-recaptcha-response'] == '') {
              $alertDanger[] = 'Please insert the Captcha';
          }
      }
      if (empty($alertDanger)) {
          User::sendResetToken($_POST['email']);
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1119: the handler checks a per-user request limit before proceeding"
  lang php
  file forgotpassword.php
  reject_review code.RateLimitRequiredOp
  code
  ```
  <?php
  if (isset($_POST['forgotpass'])) {
      $alertDanger = array();
      if (!Token::exist($_POST['token'])) {
          $alertDanger[] = TOKEN_NOT_EXIST;
      }

      if (!User::lastRequestPassword()) {
          $alertDanger[] = 'You had reached request password Limit!';
      }
      if (Xaptcha::isEnable()) {
          if (!isset($_POST['g-recaptcha-response']) || $_POST['g-recaptcha-response'] == '') {
              $alertDanger[] = 'Please insert the Captcha';
          }
      }
      if (empty($alertDanger)) {
          User::sendResetToken($_POST['email']);
      }
  }
  ```
